The lake known as '''O'Higgins''' in [[Chile]]  and '''San Martín''' in [[Argentina]] is located around coordinates {{coord|48|50|S|72|36|W|type:waterbody}} in [[Patagonia]], between the [[Aysén Region]] and the [[Santa Cruz Province (Argentina)|Santa Cruz Province]].

==General information==
[[File:Region de Aysen-Rios y Ciudades(png).png|thumb|left|O'Higgins/San Martín Lake (bottom)]]

The lake has a surface area of {{cvt|1013|km2}}, an elevation of {{cvt|250|m}} [[above mean sea level]], and a shoreline length of {{cvt|525|km}}. Viewed from above, the lake consists of a series of finger-shaped flooded valleys, of which {{cvt|554|km2}} are in Chile and {{cvt|459|km2}} in Argentina, although sources differ on the precise split, presumably reflecting water level variability. The lake is [[List of lakes by depth|the deepest]] in the Americas with a maximum depth of {{cvt|799|m}},<ref name=Minowa>{{cite journal |first1=Masahiro |last1=Minowa |first2=Marius |last2=Schaefer |first3=Pedro |last3=Skvarca |title=Effects of topography on dynamics and mass loss of lake-terminating glaciers in southern Patagonia |date=December 2023 |journal=[[Journal of Glaciology]] |volume=69 |issue=278 |pages=1580–1597 |doi=10.1017/jog.2023.42|hdl=2115/94596 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> a decrease on a 2004 figure of {{cvt|836|m}} recorded near [[O'Higgins Glacier]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.glaciologia.cl/textos/rivera.pdf | title = Mass balance investigations at Glaciar Chico, Southern Patagonia Icefield Chile | access-date = 2006-10-16 | url-status = dead | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20070818060829/http://www.glaciologia.cl/textos/rivera.pdf | archive-date = 2007-08-18 }}</ref> Its characteristic milky light-blue color comes from [[rock flour]] suspended in its waters. It is mainly fed by the [[Mayer River]] and other streams, and its outlet, the [[Pascua River]], discharges water from the lake towards the [[Pacific Ocean]] at a rate of {{convert|510|m3/s|cuft/s}}. The O'Higgins Glacier flows eastwards towards the lake, as does the [[Chico Glacier]]. Both of these glaciers are part of the [[Southern Patagonian Ice Field]] which extends for approximately {{convert|350|km}} in a north–south direction to the west of Lake O'Higgins/San Martín.

Immigrants did not settle in the arid, windy area around the lake until the 1910s, when [[United Kingdom|British]], [[Scandinavia]]ns and [[Switzerland|Swiss]] immigrants started raising [[sheep]] for wool.

The most common tourist route for visiting the lake is that between [[El Chaltén]] in Argentina and [[Villa O'Higgins]] in Chile, including a ferry through the lake on the Chilean side.

Water from O'Higgins/San Martín flows into the [[Pacific Ocean]] through the [[Pascua River]].

==History ==
The lake was divided in the [[1902 Arbitral award of the Andes between Argentina and Chile]]. Disregarding the Chilean agent Bolados and the Argentine agent Arenberg, the demarcator, [[Colonel (United Kingdom)|British Colonel]] [[Herbert Leland Crosthwait]] placed border marker 62 south of [[Lake O'Higgins/San Martín]] using a pile of stones and a paper inside a bottle, as he was short on time.

{{quote|A bottle was placed by me on pile of stones, erected as a boundary pillar between the Republics of Argentina and Chile on the south shore of lake San Martin<ref>{{cite book |author1=Alberto Marín Madrid |title=El caso del Canal Beagle biografia de esa y otras controversias |date=1988 |page=78 |url=https://www.google.es/books/edition/El_caso_del_Canal_Beagle/2iwRAAAAYAAJ?hl=es-419&gbpv=1&bsq=%22H.%20L.%20Crosthwait%22&dq=%22H.%20L.%20Crosthwait%22&printsec=frontcover |access-date=29 October 2025}}</ref>|H. L. Crosthwait, Captain R. E., February 28th 1903}}

==Names==
Being the most irregular of the lakes in the area, consisting of eight well-defined arms, the name San Martín is sometimes used to refer only to the four Argentine arms of the lake, and O'Higgins only to the four Chilean arms. Both names come from independence heroes, [[José de San Martín]] of Argentina and [[Bernardo O'Higgins]] of Chile, who [[Army of the Andes|fought together]] for the liberation of Chile, and came to be known as ''[[Libertadores|Liberators of America]]'' together with other [[South America]]n figures.

The four Argentine arms of the lake, with an area of 521&nbsp;km<sup>2</sup>, are individually named ''Cancha Rayada'', ''Chacabuco'', ''Maipú'' and ''De la Lancha'', after battles of General San Martín.

